course: dictionary
lesson: 5177
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and issue is one of them. The words around it tell you which meaning is right, so read the context first. «Family issues» means serious trouble or quarrels in a family, not a magazine or a bug.
explain: «Known issue» means a bug, which is a mistake in a computer program that has not been fixed yet. Ask: what would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Issue can also be a verb. To issue something means to give it out officially, like tickets, books or a warning. Look at the job the word does in the sentence: is it a thing, or is someone doing something?
explain: In everyday talk, «What's the issue?» means «What's wrong?» If you are not sure what a word means, check a dictionary.
fact: Issue has more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right.
fact: «Family issues» means serious trouble or quarrels in a family.
fact: «Known issue» means a bug, a mistake in a computer program that has not been fixed.
fact: As a verb, to issue something means to give it out officially, like tickets or books.
fact: An issue of a magazine or comic is one copy printed at a time, as in «issue 12».
exercise: What does «Known issue» mean? | A ticket given out at the door | *A mistake in a computer program that has not been fixed | A copy of a comic | A happy meeting of friends
exercise: What does «Family issues» mean? | *Serious trouble or quarrels in a family | A new family car | Happy days out together | Books given out at school
exercise: The referee will issue a warning. What does issue mean here? | To argue about something | An important subject people talk about | A copy of a magazine | *To give out officially
test: «Issue 12» of the comic is on sale today. What does issue mean here? | *One copy printed at a time | A problem or worry | An important subject people talk about | To give out officially
test: When someone asks «What's the issue?», what do they mean? | Where did you go? | Who is in charge here? | What time is it? | *What's wrong?
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of issue in «Known issue»? | *Swap in a meaning, such as a bug, and see if the sentence still makes sense | Ignore the word issue completely | Guess that it is a magazine | Look up only the word known
